{"parentUuid":"b6ad03ee-c035-417f-8e7f-f2900318c6f4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"id":"toolu_bdrk_01JdPjWMwnnwCTTe1TWAZhV9","input":{"prompt":"List every paper: title, year, venue, first author, and abstract if present. Verbatim.","url":"https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/search?query=proximal%20policy%20optimization%20EPANET%20water%20distribution%20pump%20valve%20control&year=2023-2026&limit=20&fields=title,year,venue,authors,abstract"},"name":"WebFetch","type":"tool_use"}],"id":"68a189af809499def55dacb1b0f73c02","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_details":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3158,"cache_read_input_tokens":256073,"output_tokens":1489,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3158},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"20260825062046406834473zvns5cOKu8eKc","type":"assistant","uuid":"1bb0593d-516b-4d54-b261-2770736d0318","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:21:10.078Z","effort":"max","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"1bb0593d-516b-4d54-b261-2770736d0318","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ff3c19c5-aaaf-4dec-bdcc-1d127ee3c9f6","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01JdPjWMwnnwCTTe1TWAZhV9","type":"tool_result","content":"All 4 records from the result set are below. Note: abstracts are present for every entry, but rather than reproducing them in full I've summarized each and kept any exact wording to short quoted fragments.\n\n---\n\n**1. \"A Smart Water Distribution Network Management Framework Based on Internet of Things and Reinforcement Learning Technologies\"**\n- Year: 2026\n- Venue: Journal of Soft Computing and Data Mining\n- First author: Ibraheem A Aidan\n- Abstract (summary): Presents a hybrid IoT–reinforcement learning architecture joining live sensing, data synchronization, adaptive control, and prediction. Distributed nodes gather pressure, flow, and energy readings, relayed over MQTT into a time-series database for feature extraction and anomaly detection, while a PPO agent learns policies to cut leakage and energy use without sacrificing hydraulic stability. Testing used EPANET 2.2 with the C-Town benchmark under 24-hour diurnal demand, comparing threshold-based SCADA (Model A), a \"hybrid PID-Model Predictive Control (PID-MPC) controller (Model C)\", and the proposed IoT-RL scheme (Model B). Reported gains for Model B: latency down 93.3% and accuracy up 22.7% versus Model A, 12.2% accuracy gain over Model C, and ~95% higher throughput; figures were generated in MATLAB from EPANET-based scripts.\n\n**2. \"Energy-Efficient Joint Scheduling of Pumps and Valves in Water Distribution Network Using Hybrid Optimization Algorithm\"**\n- Year: 2023\n- Venue: Environmental Claims Journal\n- First author: Waghmare Shwetambari Pandurang\n- Abstract (summary): Argues that utilities have historically treated \"pumping efficiency and pressure control\" as separate concerns. Since modern pumps often carry variable speed drives, outlet pressure can be regulated via speed, and inlet pressure at a pressure-reducing valve can be lowered by altering upstream pumping when no intermediate tank exists; pressure-dependent leakage should also inform schedules. The work optimizes on/off switching timetables for pumps and valves in large-scale networks to minimize energy cost while satisfying nodal demand and reducing head loss, models shaft power via quadratic polynomial fitting, and examines variable speed pumps for pressure reliability, leakage, and power draw. A \"hybrid binary dragonfly-enhanced Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) algorithm\" is evaluated under three tariffs in MATLAB with the EPANET toolkit across varying horizons and network changes.\n\n**3. \"WaterAdmin: Orchestrating Community Water Distribution Optimization via AI Agents\"**\n- Year: 2026\n- Venue: arXiv.org\n- First author: Jiaqi Wen\n- Abstract (summary): Addresses scheduling of pumps and valves in community water systems to satisfy demand at minimal energy cost. Conventional optimization assumes well-modeled conditions, yet real communities face shifting factors like human activity and weather that reshape demand and zone-level targets faster than such methods can absorb. LLM agents parse heterogeneous context well but \"are not suitable for directly producing reliable real-time control actions.\" The proposed bi-level design pairs upper-level LLM context abstraction with lower-level optimization-based control, implemented on EPANET, yielding better pressure reliability and lower energy use in dynamic settings.\n\n**4. \"Multi-algorithm reinforcement learning framework with feedforward networks for resilient water tank scheduling systems\"**\n- Year: 2026\n- Venue: Complex & Intelligent Systems\n- First author: Hee-beom Park\n- Abstract (summary): Benchmarks PPO, DQN, and A3C for water tank scheduling over multi-day horizons, with simulated demand variability, extreme scenarios, and LSTM-based temporal modeling. PPO leads on long horizons with \"up to 40% fewer pump activations and 25% fewer safety violations than DQN.\" Sensitivity analysis flags learning rate as the key hyperparameter — DQN's useful band is narrow (1×10⁻³) while PPO tolerates 1×10⁻⁵ to 3×10⁻⁴. An ablation finds the simpler DQN-FFN beating LSTM-augmented variants (cumulative reward −93.85 against −134.15 for PPO-LSTM), and under noise up to ±50 units PPO degrades 12% versus DQN's 28%. Concludes with guidance on algorithm choice, tuning, and action-space design, framed around Industry 4.0/5.0 infrastructure modernization."}]},"uuid":"57613345-8ae4-4273-92e5-1b2e25f0a8aa","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:21:48.842Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":12612,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"All 4 records from the result set are below. Note: abstracts are present for every entry, but rather than reproducing them in full I've summarized each and kept any exact wording to short quoted fragments.\n\n---\n\n**1. \"A Smart Water Distribution Network Management Framework Based on Internet of Things and Reinforcement Learning Technologies\"**\n- Year: 2026\n- Venue: Journal of Soft Computing and Data Mining\n- First author: Ibraheem A Aidan\n- Abstract (summary): Presents a hybrid IoT–reinforcement learning architecture joining live sensing, data synchronization, adaptive control, and prediction. Distributed nodes gather pressure, flow, and energy readings, relayed over MQTT into a time-series database for feature extraction and anomaly detection, while a PPO agent learns policies to cut leakage and energy use without sacrificing hydraulic stability. Testing used EPANET 2.2 with the C-Town benchmark under 24-hour diurnal demand, comparing threshold-based SCADA (Model A), a \"hybrid PID-Model Predictive Control (PID-MPC) controller (Model C)\", and the proposed IoT-RL scheme (Model B). Reported gains for Model B: latency down 93.3% and accuracy up 22.7% versus Model A, 12.2% accuracy gain over Model C, and ~95% higher throughput; figures were generated in MATLAB from EPANET-based scripts.\n\n**2. \"Energy-Efficient Joint Scheduling of Pumps and Valves in Water Distribution Network Using Hybrid Optimization Algorithm\"**\n- Year: 2023\n- Venue: Environmental Claims Journal\n- First author: Waghmare Shwetambari Pandurang\n- Abstract (summary): Argues that utilities have historically treated \"pumping efficiency and pressure control\" as separate concerns. Since modern pumps often carry variable speed drives, outlet pressure can be regulated via speed, and inlet pressure at a pressure-reducing valve can be lowered by altering upstream pumping when no intermediate tank exists; pressure-dependent leakage should also inform schedules. The work optimizes on/off switching timetables for pumps and valves in large-scale networks to minimize energy cost while satisfying nodal demand and reducing head loss, models shaft power via quadratic polynomial fitting, and examines variable speed pumps for pressure reliability, leakage, and power draw. A \"hybrid binary dragonfly-enhanced Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) algorithm\" is evaluated under three tariffs in MATLAB with the EPANET toolkit across varying horizons and network changes.\n\n**3. \"WaterAdmin: Orchestrating Community Water Distribution Optimization via AI Agents\"**\n- Year: 2026\n- Venue: arXiv.org\n- First author: Jiaqi Wen\n- Abstract (summary): Addresses scheduling of pumps and valves in community water systems to satisfy demand at minimal energy cost. Conventional optimization assumes well-modeled conditions, yet real communities face shifting factors like human activity and weather that reshape demand and zone-level targets faster than such methods can absorb. LLM agents parse heterogeneous context well but \"are not suitable for directly producing reliable real-time control actions.\" The proposed bi-level design pairs upper-level LLM context abstraction with lower-level optimization-based control, implemented on EPANET, yielding better pressure reliability and lower energy use in dynamic settings.\n\n**4. \"Multi-algorithm reinforcement learning framework with feedforward networks for resilient water tank scheduling systems\"**\n- Year: 2026\n- Venue: Complex & Intelligent Systems\n- First author: Hee-beom Park\n- Abstract (summary): Benchmarks PPO, DQN, and A3C for water tank scheduling over multi-day horizons, with simulated demand variability, extreme scenarios, and LSTM-based temporal modeling. PPO leads on long horizons with \"up to 40% fewer pump activations and 25% fewer safety violations than DQN.\" Sensitivity analysis flags learning rate as the key hyperparameter — DQN's useful band is narrow (1×10⁻³) while PPO tolerates 1×10⁻⁵ to 3×10⁻⁴. An ablation finds the simpler DQN-FFN beating LSTM-augmented variants (cumulative reward −93.85 against −134.15 for PPO-LSTM), and under noise up to ±50 units PPO degrades 12% versus DQN's 28%. Concludes with guidance on algorithm choice, tuning, and action-space design, framed around Industry 4.0/5.0 infrastructure modernization.","durationMs":38755,"url":"https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/search?query=proximal%20policy%20optimization%20EPANET%20water%20distribution%20pump%20valve%20control&year=2023-2026&limit=20&fields=title,year,venue,authors,abstract"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1bb0593d-516b-4d54-b261-2770736d0318","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
