{"parentUuid":"26bb47b8-0580-4a38-96e9-b656e5b46539","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ff3c19c5-aaaf-4dec-bdcc-1d127ee3c9f6","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Jfcw9vCFC8g97zquhnKutJ","type":"tool_result","content":"### [1] A Survey of Pressure Control Approaches in Water Supply Systems.pdf\nwater\r Review\r A Survey of Pressure Control Approaches in Water Supply Systems\r Thapelo C. Mosetlhe 1,2,* , Yskandar Hamam 1,3 , Shengzhi Du 1 and Eric Monacelli 4 1 Tshwane University of Technology, Department of Electrical Engineering, Pretoria 0183, South Africa; hamama@tut.ac.za (Y.H.); dus@tut.ac.za (S.D.) 2 Department of Electrical and Mining Engineering, University of South Africa, Florida 1709, South Africa 3 École Su\n\n### [2] Control of chaotic systems by Deep Reinforcement Learning.pdf\nDownloaded from https://royalsocietypublishing.org/ on 29 September 2025\r \r royalsocietypublishing.org/journal/rspa\r Research\r Cite this article: Bucci MA, Semeraro O, Allauzen A, Wisniewski G, Cordier L, Mathelin L. 2019 Control of chaotic systems by deep reinforcement learning. Proc. R. Soc. A 475: 20190351. http://dx.doi.org/10.1098/rspa.2019.0351 Received: 4 June 2019 Accepted: 10 October 2019\r Subject Areas: artificial in\n\n### [3] Deep Reinforcement Learning for Real-Time Optimization of Pumps in Water Distribution Systems.pdf\nDownloaded from https://iranpaper.ir\r \r https://www.tarjomano.com https://www.tarjomano.com\r \r Downloaded from ascelibrary.org by Lakehead University on 09/28/25. Copyright ASCE. For personal use only; all rights reserved.\r \r Deep Reinforcement Learning for Real-Time Optimization of Pumps in Water Distribution Systems\r Gergely Hajgato´ 1; György Paál, Ph.D., D.Sc.2; and Bálint Gyires-To´ th, Ph.D.3\r \r Abstract: Real-time \n\n### [4] Deep reinforcement learning based valve scheduling for pollution isolation in water distribution network..pdf\nhttp://www.aimspress.com/journal/MBE\r \r MBE, 17(1): 105–121. DOI: 10.3934/mbe.2020006 Received: 25 June 2019 Accepted: 18 September 2019 Published: 26 September 2019\r \r Research article\r Deep reinforcement learning based valve scheduling for pollution isolation in water distribution network\r Chengyu Hu1, Junyi Cai1, Deze Zeng1,∗, Xuesong Yan1, Wenyin Gong1 and Ling Wang2\r 1 Department of Computer Science, China university \n\n### [5] Deep reinforcement learning challenges and opportunities for urban water systems.pdf\nWater Research 253 (2024) 121145 Contents lists available at ScienceDirect\r Water Research\r journal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/watres\r \r Deep reinforcement learning challenges and opportunities for urban water systems\r Ahmed Negm , Xiandong Ma , George Aggidis *\r Lancaster University Energy Group, School of Engineering, Lancaster LA1 4YW, UK\r \r ARTICLE INFO\r Key words: Deep reinforcement learning Leakage Urban water sys\n\n### [6] Improving Water Treatment Using Reinforcement Learning.pdf\nImproving Water Treatment Using Reinforcement Learning\r by\r Puer Liu\r A thesis submitted in partial fulﬁllment of the requirements for the degree of Master of Science in\r Statistical Machine Learning\r Department of Computing Science University of Alberta\r © Puer Liu, 2022\r \r \f\n\n### [7] Improving pressure monitoring and control in order to reduce water loss in water urban public systems.pdf\nIOP Conference Series: Materials Science and Engineering\r \r PAPER • OPEN ACCESS\r \r You may also like\r \r Improving pressure monitoring and control in order to reduce water loss in water urban public systems\r \r - Untapped potential: leak reduction is the most cost-effective urban water management tool Amanda Rupiper, Joakim Weill, Ellen Bruno et al.\r \r To cite this article: F Stătescu et al 2024 IOP Conf. Ser.: Mater. Sci. En\n\n### [8] Integrated pressure control strategies for sustainable management of water distribution networks.pdf\nE3S Web of Conferences 85, 06005 (2019) EENVIRO 2018\r \r https://doi.org/10.1051/e3sconf/20198506005\r \r Integrated pressure control strategies for sustainable management of water distribution networks\r Luigi Berardi1,*, Daniele Laucelli2, Antonietta Simone2, Gianluca C. Perrone3, and Orazio Giustolisi2\r 1University “G. d’Annunzio” of Chieti-Pescara, Engineering and Geology Dept., Viale Pindaro 42, 65127, Pescara, Italy 2T\n\n### [9] Integrating the agent-based approach with hydraulic analysis of the water distribution network to a realistic micro-scale simulation of end-users in GAZ City, Iran.pdf\nJournal of Hydrology: Regional Studies 62 (2025) 102805 Contents lists available at ScienceDirect\r Journal of Hydrology: Regional Studies\r journal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/ejrh\r \r Integrating the agent-based approach with hydraulic analysis of the water distribution network to a realistic micro-scale simulation of end-users in GAZ City, Iran\r Zohre Fakhari a, Azadeh Ahmadi b,*\r a Dept. of Civil Engineering, Isfahan Un\n\n### [10] Investigation of water losses with real time pressure management as a case study in Sakarya.pdf\nwww.nature.com/scientificreports\r \r OPEN\r \r Investigation of water losses with\r real time pressure management as\r a case study in Sakarya\r Mehmet Masum Olmuştur1,2 & Fatih Uysal2\r Urban growth and limited freshwater resources have made water management increasingly critical. As cities expand and water must be transported over longer distances, the cost of supply rises, increasing the importance of minimizing water losses. \n\n### [11] Leak Management in Water Distribution Networks Through Deep Reinforcement Learning A Review.pdf\nReview\r Leak Management in Water Distribution Networks Through Deep Reinforcement Learning: A Review\r Awais Javed 1 , Wenyan Wu 1,* , Quanbin Sun 2 and Ziye Dai 3\r \r 1 College of Engineering, Birmingham City University, Birmingham B4 7RQ, UK; awais.javed@mail.bcu.ac.uk 2 College of Computing, Birmingham City University, Birmingham B4 7RQ, UK; quanbi