{"parentUuid":"40e3025c-d806-4f9a-9448-70082529965e","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"text":"مشکل پیدا شد: working directory از یک `cd` قبلی داخل `papers/` مانده. با مسیر مطلق:","type":"text"}],"id":"ae145e908a8cd605e786b1be3cc36c0f","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_details":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5844,"cache_read_input_tokens":276962,"output_tokens":583,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5844},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"202608250627546132194345vwknykGkVcIr","type":"assistant","uuid":"85b3481f-1672-45e1-8a8f-865ec6f1d1c1","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:28:05.861Z","effort":"max","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis\\papers","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"85b3481f-1672-45e1-8a8f-865ec6f1d1c1","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"id":"toolu_bdrk_01Rs9fvhVhRu21pJANE1MaEg","input":{"command":"pwd; cd /d/Thesis; pwd; echo \"=== LIEMBERGER ===\"; pdftotext -enc UTF-8 \"/d/Thesis/papers/QuantifyingTheGlobalNon-RevenueWaterProblem-Liemberger.pdf\" - 2>/dev/null | grep -aiE \"billion|Kingdom et al\" | head -6","description":"Reset working directory and extract Liemberger figures"},"name":"Bash","type":"tool_use"}],"id":"ae145e908a8cd605e786b1be3cc36c0f","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_details":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5844,"cache_read_input_tokens":276962,"output_tokens":583,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5844},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"202608250627546132194345vwknykGkVcIr","type":"assistant","uuid":"a7a814e5-f93f-4f03-b3fb-d31c27900b95","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:28:07.473Z","effort":"max","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis\\papers","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"a7a814e5-f93f-4f03-b3fb-d31c27900b95","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ff3c19c5-aaaf-4dec-bdcc-1d127ee3c9f6","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Rs9fvhVhRu21pJANE1MaEg","type":"tool_result","content":"/d/Thesis/papers\n/d/Thesis\n=== LIEMBERGER ===\nThis paper provides a long overdue update on the global non-revenue water (NRW) estimates, initially published in a World Bank publication (Kingdom et al., 2006) more than 10 years ago. The authors use a similar approach based on latest data to estimate volumes of water lost by water utilities around the world. The conclusion of this study is that the 2006 estimates were extremely conservative and that realistic NRW estimates are significantly higher. The global volume of NRW has been estimated to be 346 million cubic meters per day or 126 billion cubic meters per year. Conservatively valued with only USD 0.31 per cubic meter, the cost/value of water lost amounts to USD 39 billion per year. Not only is this an enormous financial concern, elevated NRW detracts from water utilities, in a time of increasing scarcity and climate change, from reaching their goals of full service coverage, at a reliable level of service at an affordable price.\nThe global volume of NRW has been calculated to be 346 million cubic meters per day or 126 billion cubic meters per year. To put this in some perspective, this annual volume is about 70% of the average flow of the Niger River – the principal River in West Africa, and nearly 50% of the average flow of the Ganges River in India. But more importantly, the aggregate NRW is 30% of water system input volumes across the world.\nbillion m3/year\nbillion USD/year\nThe new model has now been used to run a simulation with 2005 data to compare the result with World Bank publication (Kingdom et al., 2006) which used 2005 data. The result shows a significant difference which can be explained by the conservative assumptions made in 2006 and the fact that the first model used the supplied urban population only while this model does not differentiate between urban and rural and includes all piped water supply systems. The difference to the model published by the Asian Development Bank (Liemberger and Frauendorfer, 2010) has similar reasons.\nbillion m3 per year","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e7122047-0e32-4acc-aa10-310d07312ecd","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:28:07.796Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"/d/Thesis/papers\n/d/Thesis\n=== LIEMBERGER ===\nThis paper provides a long overdue update on the global non-revenue water (NRW) estimates, initially published in a World Bank publication (Kingdom et al., 2006) more than 10 years ago. The authors use a similar approach based on latest data to estimate volumes of water lost by water utilities around the world. The conclusion of this study is that the 2006 estimates were extremely conservative and that realistic NRW estimates are significantly higher. The global volume of NRW has been estimated to be 346 million cubic meters per day or 126 billion cubic meters per year. Conservatively valued with only USD 0.31 per cubic meter, the cost/value of water lost amounts to USD 39 billion per year. Not only is this an enormous financial concern, elevated NRW detracts from water utilities, in a time of increasing scarcity and climate change, from reaching their goals of full service coverage, at a reliable level of service at an affordable price.\nThe global volume of NRW has been calculated to be 346 million cubic meters per day or 126 billion cubic meters per year. To put this in some perspective, this annual volume is about 70% of the average flow of the Niger River – the principal River in West Africa, and nearly 50% of the average flow of the Ganges River in India. But more importantly, the aggregate NRW is 30% of water system input volumes across the world.\nbillion m3/year\nbillion USD/year\nThe new model has now been used to run a simulation with 2005 data to compare the result with World Bank publication (Kingdom et al., 2006) which used 2005 data. The result shows a significant difference which can be explained by the conservative assumptions made in 2006 and the fact that the first model used the supplied urban population only while this model does not differentiate between urban and rural and includes all piped water supply systems. The difference to the model published by the Asian Development Bank (Liemberger and Frauendorfer, 2010) has similar reasons.\nbillion m3 per year","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a7a814e5-f93f-4f03-b3fb-d31c27900b95","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
