{"parentUuid":"e5924b93-1e64-450b-926f-c38c0b0c65c5","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ff3c19c5-aaaf-4dec-bdcc-1d127ee3c9f6","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01YVj3y4hB3HMNpaWjZn6sWN","type":"tool_result","content":"=== counts ===\nPRV                5\npressure reducing  1\nthrottle           0\nTCV                0\nemitter            10\nAnytown            0\nNet3               0\nc-town             0\nuncertainty        12\nrobust             1\ngeneraliz          0\ncontinuous         0\ndiscrete           1\nPDA                8\nDDA                0\n=== valve/network context ===\nr the optimisation problem of the pump and valve scheduling are the status of the pumps and control valve settings of the PRVs in the WDN. The status of the pumps not only affects its energy consumption but also inﬂuences the press\nses level in the WDN. Valve operations, through their impact on the pressure at nodes downstream of the valves, affect the water losses cost. The pumps and valves work together to achieve the goals of water resource and energy ma\nure, and its action space was determined based on the minimum pressure requirement of consumers and the valve inlet pressure. The valve inlet pressure of the PRV was calculated using 1,000 random interaction episodes with the env","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"182c593f-5b57-4096-a87e-ca1165762958","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:28:53.963Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=== counts ===\nPRV                5\npressure reducing  1\nthrottle           0\nTCV                0\nemitter            10\nAnytown            0\nNet3               0\nc-town             0\nuncertainty        12\nrobust             1\ngeneraliz          0\ncontinuous         0\ndiscrete           1\nPDA                8\nDDA                0\n=== valve/network context ===\nr the optimisation problem of the pump and valve scheduling are the status of the pumps and control valve settings of the PRVs in the WDN. The status of the pumps not only affects its energy consumption but also inﬂuences the press\nses level in the WDN. Valve operations, through their impact on the pressure at nodes downstream of the valves, affect the water losses cost. The pumps and valves work together to achieve the goals of water resource and energy ma\nure, and its action space was determined based on the minimum pressure requirement of consumers and the valve inlet pressure. The valve inlet pressure of the PRV was calculated using 1,000 random interaction episodes with the env","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e5924b93-1e64-450b-926f-c38c0b0c65c5","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"56408d7d-f1e2-460c-933e-19a75dc3f197","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"ff3c19c5-aaaf-4dec-bdcc-1d127ee3c9f6","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Ck1v9yyPjAzodg22rrHxFC","type":"tool_result","content":"=== JAVED 2025 gaps ===\nights recent advancements in DRL applications, and discusses the challenges and future directions for leveraging this technology in water leakage management. By providing a comprehensive analysis, this review seeks to contribute to ongoing effort\nted the practical benefits of PRVs by achieving an 18% leakage reduction in the Benchmark WDS through the installation of four to six valves. Building on this, Ref. [66] introduced a multi-objective NSGA-II algorithm for optimising PRV loc\nnetworks under time-varying demand, outperforming the Nelder–Mead method on a benchmark 22-node two-pump system. Moreover, Ref. [24] utilised hybrid reinforcement learning for pump sustainability in real-world water networks. These studi\nge as a primary objective. Recurring gaps in the literature include the lack of benchmark environments, limited transferability across network topologies, and minimal real-world implementations. Overall, the review underscores the potentia\nur, I. Water Leak Detection: A Comprehensive Review of Methods, Challenges, and Future Directions. Water 2024, 16, 2975. [CrossRef] 7. Hirner, W.; Lambert, A. Losses from Water Supply Systems: Standard Terminology and Recommended Performance Meas\nReview of Deep Learning: Concepts, CNN Architectures, Challenges, Applications, Future Directions. J. Big Data 2021, 8, 1–74. [CrossRef] [PubMed] 111. Talaei Khoei, T.; Ould Slimane, H.; Kaabouch, N. Deep Learning: Systematic Review, Models, Ch\nn, M.A. Dynamic Multi-Objective Optimisation Using Deep Reinforcement Learning: Benchmark, Algorithm and an Application to Identify Vulnerable Zones Based on Water Quality. Eng. Appl. Artif. Intell. 2019, 86, 107–135. [CrossRef] 131. Nam","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"0f6d004e-2467-4fd6-8547-ef2ffb72d146","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:28:54.849Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=== JAVED 2025 gaps ===\nights recent advancements in DRL applications, and discusses the challenges and future directions for leveraging this technology in water leakage management. By providing a comprehensive analysis, this review seeks to contribute to ongoing effort\nted the practical benefits of PRVs by achieving an 18% leakage reduction in the Benchmark WDS through the installation of four to six valves. Building on this, Ref. [66] introduced a multi-objective NSGA-II algorithm for optimising PRV loc\nnetworks under time-varying demand, outperforming the Nelder–Mead method on a benchmark 22-node two-pump system. Moreover, Ref. [24] utilised hybrid reinforcement learning for pump sustainability in real-world water networks. These studi\nge as a primary objective. Recurring gaps in the literature include the lack of benchmark environments, limited transferability across network topologies, and minimal real-world implementations. Overall, the review underscores the potentia\nur, I. Water Leak Detection: A Comprehensive Review of Methods, Challenges, and Future Directions. Water 2024, 16, 2975. [CrossRef] 7. Hirner, W.; Lambert, A. Losses from Water Supply Systems: Standard Terminology and Recommended Performance Meas\nReview of Deep Learning: Concepts, CNN Architectures, Challenges, Applications, Future Directions. J. Big Data 2021, 8, 1–74. [CrossRef] [PubMed] 111. Talaei Khoei, T.; Ould Slimane, H.; Kaabouch, N. Deep Learning: Systematic Review, Models, Ch\nn, M.A. Dynamic Multi-Objective Optimisation Using Deep Reinforcement Learning: Benchmark, Algorithm and an Application to Identify Vulnerable Zones Based on Water Quality. Eng. Appl. Artif. Intell. 2019, 86, 107–135. [CrossRef] 131. Nam","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"56408d7d-f1e2-460c-933e-19a75dc3f197","session_id":"a4c