{"parentUuid":"a9d5991e-64f1-4060-a521-3e60f36a8980","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"id":"toolu_bdrk_01GYDA6WJPSYRE8bAU3wz3R2","input":{"command":"F=\"/d/Thesis/papers/Multi-agent reinforcement learning framework for real-time scheduling of pump and valve in water distribution networks.pdf\"; echo \"=== NETWORK IDENTITY ===\"; pdftotext -enc UTF-8 \"$F\" - 2>/dev/null | grep -aoiE \".{130}(case network|the network (is|used|has)|junctions|nodes,|benchmark|Jowitt|two-loop|real network).{130}\" | head -6","description":"Identify Hu MARL test network"},"name":"Bash","type":"tool_use"}],"id":"972248a01810a84a3dd7b482ed1a93ee","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_details":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":33998,"cache_read_input_tokens":299615,"output_tokens":2506,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":33998},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"202608250644428433035466spcqwjN1PGs5","type":"assistant","uuid":"5484a602-1998-419f-a183-4c5e18885514","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:45:24.400Z","effort":"max","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"a9d5991e-64f1-4060-a521-3e60f36a8980","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6785fb76-59d2-4c04-ae7f-c3e6eb641817","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01AaD1gWuFY2FPzButvFriYr","type":"tool_result","content":"**Note:** Two titles and all four abstracts exceed the length I can reproduce verbatim, so those are quoted in part and otherwise summarized in my own words. (The response set reports 822,336 total results, 4 items returned.)\n\n---\n\n### 1. Cyriac & Chacko\n- **Title:** \"Deep Reinforcement Learning for Pressure Optimization in Water Distribution Networks with Multiple Pumping Stations\"\n- **Authors:** Unni Cyriac (first; ORCID 0009-0001-2318-4282, authenticated), Sibi Chacko (additional) — both Heriot-Watt Univ., United Arab Emirates\n- **Journal:** \"Journal of Water Resources Planning and Management\"\n- **Published:** July 1, 2026 (2026-07-01)\n- **DOI:** 10.1061/jwrmd5.wreng-7303\n- **Abstract:** None included in the record. Type: journal-article.\n\n---\n\n### 2. León-Celi et al.\n- **Title:** \"A Methodology for the Optimization of Flow Rate Injection to Looped Water Distribution Networks through Multiple\"… — the title closes with a reference to pumping stations.\n- **Authors:** Christian León-Celi (first), Pedro Iglesias-Rey (ORCID 0000-0001-8300-3255), F. Martínez-Solano (0000-0002-8140-5960) — all Universitat Politècnica de Valencia, Spain; Daniel Mora-Melia (0000-0002-6191-7299) — Universidad de Talca, Chile\n- **Journal:** \"Water\"\n- **Published:** December 6, 2016 (2016-12-06)\n- **DOI:** 10.3390/w8120575\n- **Abstract (summary):** The authors argue a network works best when demands are met with minimum energy while the required minimum pressure is preserved. They propose optimizing injected flow rate using \"the setpoint curve concept,\" assessing the flow and pressure head each pumping station must deliver at lowest power draw. Two variants are offered: a discrete method testing a finite set of station combinations, and a continuous one where \"Hooke–Jeeves and Nelder–Mead algorithms are used.\" Hydraulics and the objective function are computed with \"EPANET and its Toolkit,\" and two case studies are discussed.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Joo, Jeong & Kang\n- **Title:** \"Deep Reinforcement Learning for Multi-Objective Real-Time Pump Operation in Rainwater Pumping Stations\"\n- **Authors:** Jin-Gul Joo (first; Dongshin University, Naju), In-Seon Jeong (Chonnam National University, Gwangju), Seung-Ho Kang (Dongshin University, Naju) — Republic of Korea\n- **Journal:** \"Water\"\n- **Published:** November 26, 2024 (2024-11-26)\n- **DOI:** 10.3390/w16233398\n- **Abstract (summary):** Rainwater pumping stations near cities or farmland limit flooding by switching on pumps of differing capacity according to live inflow, but the authors state that \"rule-based pump operations that monitor only basin water levels\" are frequently inadequate. Besides holding peak water level down, operators want fewer on/off cycles, since \"Reducing pump switch frequency lowers the likelihood of mechanical failure\" and cuts maintenance cost. Their deep reinforcement learning controller uses only currently observable variables — rainfall, inflow, storage volume, basin level, outflow. Training rainfall of varied return periods and durations was synthesized with the Huff method, and SWMM was set up to represent the Gasan station in Geumcheon-gu, Seoul; results were benchmarked against the station's existing rule-based scheme.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Li et al. (preprint)\n- **Title:** \"Optimization of DMAs design and cooperative pressure reducing valves and pumping stations to achieve leakage control\"… — continues with an energy-saving strategy for water distribution networks.\n- **Authors:** HongYan Li (first), Wentao Shi, Zifeng Chang, Feng Zhang, Jianguo Cui (all Taiyuan University of Technology); Xulong Lu (Institute of Architectural Engineering Design CNNC Co.7 Research and Design Institute Co); Libo Mao (Shanxi Dadi Environment Investment Holdings Co)\n- **Journal/container:** None listed; record type is \"posted-content\" (preprint)\n- **Published:** June 4, 2024 (2024-06-04)\n- **DOI:** 10.21203/rs.3.rs-4399025/v1\n- **Abstract (summary):** Addressing leakage and energy use in urban water distribution networks, the paper offers a new workflow for creating district metered areas and managing pressure. Step one pairs NSGA-III with spectral clustering — weighing partition cost, operational difficulty, post-partition water quality, and supply capacity — and uses multicriteria decision making to select layouts and boundary pipes. Step two applies NSGA-II to site valves and flow meters on those boundary pipes, judged on equipment cost, water quality, and leakage reduction. Finally, pressure reducing valves at DMA inlets are co-scheduled with fixed- and variable-speed pumps via a genetic algorithm. Tested on the SH town network, the approach reportedly cut leakage and pumping energy and also \"improve the water quality of networks.\""}]},"uuid":"fa001206-9a90-4e4a-ad57-e2d2d6453ee8","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:46:32.813Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":9531,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Note:** Two titles and all four abstracts exceed the length I can reproduce verbatim, so those are quoted in part and otherwise summarized in my own words. (The response set reports 822,336 total results, 4 items returned.)\n\n---\n\n### 1. Cyriac & Chacko\n- **Title:** \"Deep Reinforcement Learning for Pressure Optimization in Water Distribution Networks with Multiple Pumping Stations\"\n- **Authors:** Unni Cyriac (first; ORCID 0009-0001-2318-4282, authenticated), Sibi Chacko (additional) — both Heriot-Watt Univ., United Arab Emirates\n- **Journal:** \"Journal of Water Resources Planning and Management\"\n- **Published:** July 1, 2026 (2026-07-01)\n- **DOI:** 10.1061/jwrmd5.wreng-7303\n- **Abstract:** None included in the record. Type: journal-article.\n\n---\n\n### 2. León-Celi et al.\n- **Title:** \"A Methodology for the Optimization of Flow Rate Injection to Looped Water Distribution Networks through Multiple\"… — the title closes with a reference to pumping stations.\n- **Authors:** Christian León-Celi (first), Pedro Iglesias-Rey (ORCID 0000-0001-8300-3255), F. Martínez-Solano (0000-0002-8140-5960) — all Universitat Politècnica de Valencia, Spain; Daniel Mora-Melia (0000-0002-6191-7299) — Universidad de Talca, Chile\n- **Journal:** \"Water\"\n- **Published:** December 6, 2016 (2016-12-06)\n- **DOI:** 10.3390/w8120575\n- **Abstract (summary):** The authors argue a network works best when demands are met with minimum energy while the required minimum pressure is preserved. They propose optimizing injected flow rate using \"the setpoint curve concept,\" assessing the flow and pressure head each pumping station must deliver at lowest power draw. Two variants are offered: a discrete method testing a finite set of station combinations, and a continuous one where \"Hooke–Jeeves and Nelder–Mead algorithms are used.\" Hydraulics and the objective function are computed with \"EPANET and its Toolkit,\" and two case studies are discussed.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Joo, Jeong & Kang\n- **Title:** \"Deep Reinforcement Learning for Multi-Objective Real-Time Pump Operation in Rainwater Pumping Stations\"\n- **Authors:** Jin-Gul Joo (first; Dongshin University, Naju), In-Seon Jeong (Chonnam National University, Gwangju), Seung-Ho Kang (Dongshin University, Naju) — Republic of Korea\n- **Journal:** \"Water\"\n- **Published:** November 26, 2024 (2024-11-26)\n- **DOI:** 10.3390/w16233398\n- **Abstract (summary):** Rainwater pumping stations near cities or farmland limit flooding by switching on pumps of differing capacity according to live inflow, but the authors state that \"rule-based pump operations that monitor only basin water levels\" are frequently inadequate. Besides holding peak water level down, operators want fewer on/off cycles, since \"Reducing pump switch frequency lowers the likelihood of mechanical failure\" and cuts maintenance cost. Their deep reinforcement learning controller uses only currently observable variables — rainfall, inflow, storage volume, basin level, outflow. Training rainfall of varied return periods and durations was synthesized with the Huff method, and SWMM was set up to represent the Gasan station in Geumcheon-gu, Seoul; results were benchmarked against the station's existing rule-based scheme.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Li et al. (preprint)\n- **Title:** \"Optimization of DMAs design and cooperative pressure reducing valves and pumping stations to achieve leakage control\"… — continues with an energy-saving strategy for water distribution networks.\n- **Authors:** HongYan Li (first), Wentao Shi, Zifeng Chang, Feng Zhang, Jianguo Cui (all Taiyuan University of Technology); Xulong Lu (Institute of Architectural Engineering Design CNNC Co.7 Research and Design Institute Co); Libo Mao (Shanxi Dadi Environment Investment Holdings Co)\n- **Journal/container:** None listed; record type is \"posted-content\" (preprint)\n- **Published:** June 4, 2024 (2024-06-04)\n- **DOI:** 10.21203/rs.3.rs-4399025/v1\n- **Abstract (summary):** Addressing leakage and energy use in urban water distribution networks, the paper offers a new workflow for creating district metered areas and managing pressure. Step one pairs NSGA-III with spectral clustering — weighing partition cost, operational difficulty, post-partition water quality, and supply capacity — and uses multicriteria decision making to select layouts and boundary pipes. Step two applies NSGA-II to site valves and flow meters on those boundary pipes, judged on equipment cost, water quality, and leakage reduction. Finally, pressure reducing valves at DMA inlets are co-scheduled with fixed- and variable-speed pumps via a genetic algorithm. Tested on the SH town network, the approach reportedly cut leakage and pumping energy and also \"improve the water quality of networks.\"","durationMs":58818,"url":"https://api.crossref.org/works?query.bibliographic=Deep+Reinforcement+Learning+Pressure+Optimization+Water+Distribution+Networks+Multiple+Pumping+Stations&rows=4&select=title,author,container-title,published,DOI,abstract,type"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a9d5991e-64f1-4060-a521-3e60f36a8980","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
