{"parentUuid":"9d76c7a2-de5a-4e88-85de-5f993763d4ce","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"text":"**دو تأیید + یک کشف مهم:**\n\n**۱. تأیید شد:** EPANET 2.2 صریحاً «the ability to use **pressure dependent demands** in hydraulic analyses» را اضافه کرده.\n\n**۲. 🟢 کشف مهم که احتمالاً نمی‌دانستید:** نسخه **EPANET 2.3** پشتیبانی **بومی FAVAD pipe leakage** و «positional control valves» اضافه کرده. این دقیقاً **تله تداخل emitter** را که در Phase 1.5 علامت زدم حل می‌کند — چون PDA در حلگر و نشت FAVAD جداگانه مدل می‌شوند.\n\n**۳. ⚠️ بازتفسیر بحرانی یافته #۴:** جدول ۵-۳ Negm: PPO با **فقط ۲۰,۰۰۰ timestep** آموزش دیده (lr=0.0003، γ=0.99، clip=0.2، پیش‌فرض‌های SB3).\n\nادامه — جدول نتایج و مشخصات شبکه:","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_bdrk_zx6vf52q7sd6cxxnibuhlhqjwt67mv3i272lllkhunfqpmsnimoa","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_details":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":309918,"cache_read_input_tokens":49289,"output_tokens":2749,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":309918},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"20260825065453569233751hqf6dWmd2jGtG","type":"assistant","uuid":"76c57912-ba0a-4f5f-b4c3-1e129340b165","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:55:33.400Z","effort":"max","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"76c57912-ba0a-4f5f-b4c3-1e129340b165","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"id":"toolu_bdrk_01J5KFM2hmzQJmWuMeLtVc5c","input":{"command":"F=\"/d/Thesis/papers/Water pressure optimisation for leakage management using deep reinforcement learning.pdf\"; echo \"=== TABLE 5-4 Key results Jowitt & Xu ===\"; pdftotext -layout -enc UTF-8 \"$F\" - 2>/dev/null | grep -a -A22 \"Table 5-4 Key results\" | head -30","description":"Extract Negm Table 5-4 algorithm ranking"},"name":"Bash","type":"tool_use"}],"id":"msg_bdrk_zx6vf52q7sd6cxxnibuhlhqjwt67mv3i272lllkhunfqpmsnimoa","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_details":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":309918,"cache_read_input_tokens":49289,"output_tokens":2749,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":309918},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"20260825065453569233751hqf6dWmd2jGtG","type":"assistant","uuid":"09e987cf-b217-4433-a7d3-79add5a7d013","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:55:35.207Z","effort":"max","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"09e987cf-b217-4433-a7d3-79add5a7d013","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6785fb76-59d2-4c04-ae7f-c3e6eb641817","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01J5KFM2hmzQJmWuMeLtVc5c","type":"tool_result","content":"=== TABLE 5-4 Key results Jowitt & Xu ===\nTable 5-4 Key results – Jowitt & Xu ........................................................................................... 131\nTable 5-5 Summary of network parameters ............................................................................. 135\nTable 5-6 DRL agent hyperparameters - SZ08 ........................................................................... 137\nTable 5-7 Key results - SZ08....................................................................................................... 142\nTable 6-1 Benchmark pipe and amended node data. ............................................................... 152\nTable 6-2 Key results - Jowitt & Xu ............................................................................................ 156\nTable 6-3 Key results - SZ08....................................................................................................... 167\n\n                                                        XVI\n\f   1. Introduction\n\nWater, earth’s most essential resource, dictates the health of our societies yet failures in water\ndistribution networks (WDNs) have amounted to 51 litres of leakage per person per day in the\nUnited Kingdom (OFWAT, 2022). This amounts to 23% of all distributed water in 2022 (OFWAT,\n2022). This loss of water is reflected in a loss of revenue to water companies sector wide. With\nthe diverse nature of urban cities, rising customer demand patterns, varying landscape\ntopologies and seasonal weather trends; managing WDNs has proved a complex task. Hence\nwhy, water companies and utilities constantly explore new avenues to incorporate and test\nnew methods to better manage their water practices.\n\nIn this thesis, we introduce the use of deep reinforcement learning as a technique to manage\npressure. The aim is to minimise the effects of background and burst leakage events without\nviolating nodal pressure limits. We believe the use of DRL algorithms will reduce the\n--\n\fTable 5-4 Key results – Jowitt & Xu\n\nAlgorithm  Average  Average          Average     Carbon     Training  Test\n           Reward   Water Saved      Pressure    Emissions  Time (s)  Time (s)\nNM                  (%)              Violations  Reduction\nPSO        965.7                                 (KgCO2)    NA        233","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"2110dceb-7972-43c9-b10c-331ff0c1c6fe","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:55:37.975Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=== TABLE 5-4 Key results Jowitt & Xu ===\nTable 5-4 Key results – Jowitt & Xu ........................................................................................... 131\nTable 5-5 Summary of network parameters ............................................................................. 135\nTable 5-6 DRL agent hyperparameters - SZ08 ........................................................................... 137\nTable 5-7 Key results - SZ08....................................................................................................... 142\nTable 6-1 Benchmark pipe and amended node data. ............................................................... 152\nTable 6-2 Key results - Jowitt & Xu ............................................................................................ 156\nTable 6-3 Key results - SZ08....................................................................................................... 167\n\n                                                        XVI\n\f   1. Introduction\n\nWater, earth’s most essential resource, dictates the health of our societies yet failures in water\ndistribution networks (WDNs) have amounted to 51 litres of leakage per person per day in the\nUnited Kingdom (OFWAT, 2022). This amounts to 23% of all distributed water in 2022 (OFWAT,\n2022). This loss of water is reflected in a loss of revenue to water companies sector wide. With\nthe diverse nature of urban cities, rising customer demand patterns, varying landscape\ntopologies and seasonal weather trends; managing WDNs has proved a complex task. Hence\nwhy, water companies and utilities constantly explore new avenues to incorporate and test\nnew methods to better manage their water practices.\n\nIn this thesis, we introduce the use of deep reinforcement learning as a technique to manage\npressure. The aim is to minimise the effects of background and burst leakage events without\nviolating nodal pressure limits. We believe the use of DRL algorithms will reduce the\n--\n\fTable 5-4 Key results – Jowitt & Xu\n\nAlgorithm  Average  Average          Average     Carbon     Training  Test\n           Reward   Water Saved      Pressure    Emissions  Time (s)  Time (s)\nNM                  (%)              Violations  Reduction\nPSO        965.7                                 (KgCO2)    NA        233","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"09e987cf-b217-4433-a7d3-79add5a7d013","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
