e\nUnited Kingdom (OFWAT, 2022). This amounts to 23% of all distributed water in 2022 (OFWAT,\n2022). This loss of water is reflected in a loss of revenue to water companies sector wide. With\nthe diverse nature of urban cities, rising customer demand patterns, varying landscape\ntopologies and seasonal weather trends; managing WDNs has proved a complex task. Hence\nwhy, water companies and utilities constantly explore new avenues to incorporate and test\n--\nTable 5-5 Summary of network parameters\n\nName  Junctions Pipes Valves Pumps Tanks/Reservoirs DMAs Size\n\n\n=== timesteps mentions ===\ng of the parameterise policy (∇θ log (πθ (at|st))) for timesteps (t) in episode length (T). This is the simplest policy gradient; ther\nh new setting introduced each hour thus forming 24 distinct timesteps. This matches the operations of an advanced WDN control room as most \nvents iteratively. This method allows us to simulate the 24 timesteps for each hour of the day with an option of altering our actuators and\nment is designed to simulate 24h steps to model the 24-hour timesteps in the demand pattern used. This allows the agent to change each PRV \nst))) multiplied by the expected sum of returns (R(τ)) for timesteps (t) in episode length (T). KL clipping will be optimised along with h\n subjected to three full training episodes consisting of 24 timesteps each modelling an hour of the customers demand patterns. The resultin","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cb74ce38-610c-4574-ae05-192f18d5d8bf","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
