{"parentUuid":"a83f18db-3536-48ef-9375-59d405f90e48","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6785fb76-59d2-4c04-ae7f-c3e6eb641817","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_0113de3Bi46aLExpJkY5FcE7","type":"tool_result","content":"In retrospect, policy driven methods (TRPO, PPO, Recurrent PPO, ARS) couldn’t match their\r\nhybrid alternatives. This can be explained with the policy-driven methods’ lower stability and\r\nsensitivity to changes in the environment due to the absence of the value function. TRPO\r\ntackles this instability through trust region methods. However, the trust region constraints led\r\nto conservative policy updates and hindered the algorithm’s ability to explore better solutions.\r\nThis affected the algorithm’s performance making it less beneficial than hybrid methods yet an\r\nimprovement on other policy-driven methods (PPO, Recurrent PPO) with 800.5 episodic\r\nrewards. The rewards were manifested through a great performance in leakage minimisation\r\n(73.2%) at the expense of pressure violations (264). Comparable performances were achieved\r\nby PPO and Recurrent PPO agents that exploited the leakage objective at the expense of the\r\npressure violation objective both saving the highest amount of water at 73.4% and violating\r\n304 and 300 pressure limits respectively. Their performances in leakage minimisation have\r\nalso translated in carbon emissions reduction placing them joint first with episodic reductions\r\nof 302.5kg of CO2. Recurrent PPO’s ability to minimise pressure violations better placed it\r\nahead of its predecessor PPO. This could be due to the long short-term memory (LSTM)\r\nfunctionality placed on the neural networks however their results are too close to draw a valid\r\nconclusion. Finally, the most effective policy driven agent was the augmented random search\r\n(ARS) algorithm. ARS managed to provide a better trade-off between saving water and\r\npressure regulation by focusing more on pressure regulation than its neighbours PPO and\r\nrecurrent PPO. As a result, it developed a policy that produced lower violations (78) at the\r\nexpense of lower water saved (65.5%). ARS learns through random search on the\r\nparameterised policy equation making it more sample efficient but slightly less stable.\r\nNevertheless, its simple exploration strategy has waged well in the case study.\r\n\r\n    5.3. Northumbrian Water Network\r\n\r\nIn contrast to the Jowitt & Xu network, the second case study tackles a much larger system\r\nprovided from a real network under the supervision of Northumbrian Water Living (NWL).\r\nNWL has agreed to the use of their hydraulic data and models to simulate the control of\r\npressure reducing valves (PRVs) and the throttle control valves (TCVs) for the purpose of\r\npressure management and leakage reduction. Whilst the Jowitt & Xu network represents the\r\nsize of approximately 1-2 DMAs in what is considered a medium sized WDN problem; NWL’s\r\nSZ08 model represents a full water distribution system consisting of 19 DMAS and records of\r\n36 unique demand patterns for residential building, police stations, hospitals, commerce and\r\nmore. The complexity of this case study increases exponentially when comparing network sizes\r\nhence increasing computational load and testing the limits of the optimisation algorithms\r\n\r\n                                                        134\r\n\ftested. A summary of network parameters of SZ08 and Jowitt & Xu is shown below in table 5-\r\n5.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"dd465f61-bc82-49b1-acbf-1b1f75acfb55","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:57:10.982Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"In retrospect, policy driven methods (TRPO, PPO, Recurrent PPO, ARS) couldn’t match their\r\nhybrid alternatives. This can be explained with the policy-driven methods’ lower stability and\r\nsensitivity to changes in the environment due to the absence of the value function. TRPO\r\ntackles this instability through trust region methods. However, the trust region constraints led\r\nto conservative policy updates and hindered the algorithm’s ability to explore better solutions.\r\nThis affected the algorithm’s performance making it less beneficial than hybrid methods yet an\r\nimprovement on other policy-driven methods (PPO, Recurrent PPO) with 800.5 episodic\r\nrewards. The rewards were manifested through a great performance in leakage minimisation\r\n(73.2%) at the expense of pressure violations (264). Comparable performances were achieved\r\nby PPO and Recurrent PPO agents that exploited the leakage objective at the expense of the\r\npressure violation objective both saving the highest amount of water at 73.4% and violating\r\n304 and 300 pressure limits respectively. Their performances in leakage minimisation have\r\nalso translated in carbon emissions reduction placing them joint first with episodic reductions\r\nof 302.5kg of CO2. Recurrent PPO’s ability to minimise pressure violations better placed it\r\nahead of its predecessor PPO. This could be due to the long short-term memory (LSTM)\r\nfunctionality placed on the neural networks however their results are too close to draw a valid\r\nconclusion. Finally, the most effective policy driven agent was the augmented random search\r\n(ARS) algorithm. ARS managed to provide a better trade-off between saving water and\r\npressure regulation by focusing more on pressure regulation than its neighbours PPO and\r\nrecurrent PPO. As a result, it developed a policy that produced lower violations (78) at the\r\nexpense of lower water saved (65.5%). ARS learns through random search on the\r\nparameterised policy equation making it more sample efficient but slightly less stable.\r\nNevertheless, its simple exploration strategy has waged well in the case study.\r\n\r\n    5.3. Northumbrian Water Network\r\n\r\nIn contrast to the Jowitt & Xu network, the second case study tackles a much larger system\r\nprovided from a real network under the supervision of Northumbrian Water Living (NWL).\r\nNWL has agreed to the use of their hydraulic data and models to simulate the control of\r\npressure reducing valves (PRVs) and the throttle control valves (TCVs) for the purpose of\r\npressure management and leakage reduction. Whilst the Jowitt & Xu network represents the\r\nsize of approximately 1-2 DMAs in what is considered a medium sized WDN problem; NWL’s\r\nSZ08 model represents a full water distribution system consisting of 19 DMAS and records of\r\n36 unique demand patterns for residential building, police stations, hospitals, commerce and\r\nmore. The complexity of this case study increases exponentially when comparing network sizes\r\nhence increasing computational load and testing the limits of the optimisation algorithms\r\n\r\n                                                        134\r\n\ftested. A summary of network parameters of SZ08 and Jowitt & Xu is shown below in table 5-\r\n5.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a83f18db-3536-48ef-9375-59d405f90e48","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"dd465f61-bc82-49b1-acbf-1b1f75acfb55","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"","type":"thinking"}],"id":"msg_bdrk_72p3rl6kwmnimamuki5zwp5geuxtpy6c3hxiqwhu4bctf7v4iwbq","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_details":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7775,"cache_read_input_tokens":359207,"output_tokens":2970,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7775},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"20260825065713763380042fcmflZw56hQwU","type":"assistant","uuid":"18c8fdb7-15a9-4721-bc56-6a04cd072134","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:57:46.326Z","effort":"max","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"18c8fdb7-15a9-4721-bc56-6a04cd072134","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"text":"**🔴 تصحیح بزرگ — یافته #۴ و CE-4 من در Phase 1.5 نادرست/ناقص بود، و CE-13 اکنون تأیید مستقیم شد.**\n\nنقل مستقیم از Negm (ص۱۳۰، منبع اصلی):\n\n> «**PPO and Recurrent PPO models yielded the highest % of water saved at 73.4% and the highest carbon emissions reduction with 302.5 kgCO2**… However, this came at the expense of pressure violations that accumulated to the values of 300 for Recurrent PPO and **304 for PPO**. **In contrast, DE showed the best pressure violations minimisation with 46 violations and the best average episodic reward 995.2**.»\n\n> «PPO and Recurrent PPO agents that **exploited the leakage objective at the expense of the pressure violation objective**»\n\n**یعنی:** PPO روی هدف اصلی پروژه شما (کاهش نشت) **برنده شد (۷۳.۴٪)** — اما با **۳۰۴ نقض فشار**، در حالی که DE کلاسیک با **۴۶ نقض** و بهترین پاداش کل (۹۹۵.۲) برنده تعادل شد.\n\n**واژه «exploited» از خودِ Negm است** — این **Reward Hacking مستند**‌شده است. CE-13 من که «Inference» علامت خورده بود، اکنون **Direct Evidence** است.\n\nتلاش نهایی برای اعداد کامل جدول ۵-۴:","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_bdrk_72p3rl6kwmnimamuki5zwp5geuxtpy6c3hxiqwhu4bctf7v4iwbq","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_details":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7775,"cache_read_input_tokens":359207,"output_tokens":2970,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7775},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"20260825065713763380042fcmflZw56hQwU","type":"assistant","uuid":"5530ce9f-4239-48e1-a171-1fa2b9a30174","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:57:56.022Z","effort":"max","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"5530ce9f-4239-48e1-a171-1fa2b9a30174","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"id":"toolu_bdrk_01P8xAmbNweNZbreSvjfgQak","input":{"command":"F=\"/d/Thesis/papers/Water pressure optimisation for leakage management using deep reinforcement learning.pdf\"; pdftotext -layout -f 148 -l 148 -enc UTF-8 \"$F\" - 2>/dev/null | head -60","description":"Extract full Negm Table 5-4 data rows"},"name":"Bash","type":"tool_use"}],"id":"msg_bdrk_72p3rl6kwmnimamuki5zwp5geuxtpy6c3hxiqwhu4bctf7v4iwbq","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_details":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7775,"cache_read_input_tokens":359207,"output_tokens":2970,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7775},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"20260825065713763380042fcmflZw56hQwU","type":"assistant","uuid":"b250ea96-1bd9-4d03-99b7-0681f896a94b","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:57:57.205Z","effort":"max","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"b250ea96-1bd9-4d03-99b7-0681f896a94b","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6785fb76-59d2-4c04-ae7f-c3e6eb641817","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01P8xAmbNweNZbreSvjfgQak","type":"tool_result","content":"Table 5-4 Key results – Jowitt & Xu\r\n\r\nAlgorithm  Average  Average          Average     Carbon     Training  Test\r\n           Reward   Water Saved      Pressure    Emissions  Time (s)  Time (s)\r\nNM                  (%)              Violations  Reduction\r\nPSO        965.7                                 (KgCO2)    NA        233\r\nDE         994.5    66.0             49          273.2      NA        1274\r\nARS-       995.2    65.9             48          272.8      NA        676\r\nTuned      943.5    65.9             46          269.2      335       26.6\r\nSAC-                65.5             78          270.9\r\nTuned      975.0                                            850       27\r\nTQC-                64.2             49          269.5\r\nTuned      976.3                                            1892.3    24.8\r\nTRPO                65.2             50          270.1\r\nPPO        800.5                                            259       27\r\nRecurrent  780.0    73.2             264         301.7      216       26.6\r\nPPO        798.4    73.4             304         302.5      401       27\r\nDDPG                73.4             300         302.5\r\nA2C        525.0                                            558       22.1\r\n           822.5    44.1             48          181.1      321.5     22.7\r\n                    73.2             300         301.8\r\n\r\n    5.2.2. Discussions\r\nIn this section, we discuss and evaluate the results of the Jowitt & Xu network for the\r\nbackground leakage case study presented in section 5.2.1. The main objectives of this case\r\nstudy was to minimise leakage through pressure valve control and minimising pressure\r\nviolations for the benchmarked test network presented in (Jowitt and Xu, 1990).\r\n\r\nInitial Simulation\r\nWhen challenged with controlling PRVs in the Jowitt & Xu network, all optimisation algorithms\r\nperformed favourably however results varied greatly. The DRL algorithm’s lower performance\r\ncan be explained by their untuned parameters, yet they remain the most attractive option due\r\nto their high computational efficiency. All untuned DRL algorithms performed less favourably\r\nthan the three benchmark optimisation algorithms in the initial simulation. Nevertheless, the\r\n\r\n                                                        131","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"38f31812-a503-4094-aded-417af75d7f35","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:57:57.456Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"Table 5-4 Key results – Jowitt & Xu\r\n\r\nAlgorithm  Average  Average          Average     Carbon     Training  Test\r\n           Reward   Water Saved      Pressure    Emissions  Time (s)  Time (s)\r\nNM                  (%)              Violations  Reduction\r\nPSO        965.7                                 (KgCO2)    NA        233\r\nDE         994.5    66.0             49          273.2      NA        1274\r\nARS-       995.2    65.9             48          272.8      NA        676\r\nTuned      943.5    65.9             46          269.2      335       26.6\r\nSAC-                65.5             78          270.9\r\nTuned      975.0                                            850       27\r\nTQC-                64.2             49          269.5\r\nTuned      976.3                                            1892.3    24.8\r\nTRPO                65.2             50          270.1\r\nPPO        800.5                                            259       27\r\nRecurrent  780.0    73.2             264         301.7      216       26.6\r\nPPO        798.4    73.4             304         302.5      401       27\r\nDDPG                73.4             300         302.5\r\nA2C        525.0                                            558       22.1\r\n           822.5    44.1             48          181.1      321.5     22.7\r\n                    73.2             300         301.8\r\n\r\n    5.2.2. Discussions\r\nIn this section, we discuss and evaluate the results of the Jowitt & Xu network for the\r\nbackground leakage case study presented in section 5.2.1. The main objectives of this case\r\nstudy was to minimise leakage through pressure valve control and minimising pressure\r\nviolations for the benchmarked test network presented in (Jowitt and Xu, 1990).\r\n\r\nInitial Simulation\r\nWhen challenged with controlling PRVs in the Jowitt & Xu network, all optimisation algorithms\r\nperformed favourably however results varied greatly. The DRL algorithm’s lower performance\r\ncan be explained by their untuned parameters, yet they remain the most attractive option due\r\nto their high computational efficiency. All untuned DRL algorithms performed less favourably\r\nthan the three benchmark optimisation algorithms in the initial simulation. Nevertheless, the\r\n\r\n                                                        131","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b250ea96-1bd9-4d03-99b7-0681f896a94b","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"38f31812-a503-4094-aded-417af75d7f35","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"I'm working through the data carefully, using the prose descriptions as my reference point since the table layout got shifted during extraction. I need to cross-check the performance metrics for PPO and Recurrent PPO against what the text actually says to make sure I have the right numbers. The table rows are clearly misaligned with column shifts and interleaved tuned/untuned variants making it impossible to map correctly. I'll stick with what the prose explicitly confirms: PPO achieved the best leakage rate at 73.4% but incurred 304 violations, while DE delivered the best violation count at 46 and the highest reward at 995.2. The full table needs visual inspection to resolve the alignment issues, but these prose-verified metrics are solid. There's definitely a row alignment issue here that makes it hard to pin down whether DE or ARS-Tuned actually achieved 995.2 reward, and whether the 46 violations belong to DE or the tuned variant. But the core finding holds regardless—a classical metaheuristic paired with tuning reached roughly 46-50 violations with ~995 reward, while PPO ballooned to 304 violations. That's the critical contrast, so I should flag this as pending clarification while noting the qualitative conclusion is solid either way.\n\nI also caught an important quote: untuned DRL algorithms underperformed the benchmark optimizers initially, and Table 5-4 shows the tuned results. There's also ambiguity around DDPG (73.2%/300 or 44.1%/48) and A2C (525.0 or 822.5 reward) that I need to mark as uncertain. I have enough evidence to separate prose-verified facts from table alignment issues. Now I should verify the Jowitt & Xu network specs in Table 5-5 as a priority-2 check with my remaining verification budget. I'll tackle both the table extraction and the citation verification together.","type":"thinking"}],"id":"msg_bdrk_mjgyoc4ivoqnrkbij3vs6ruvaxjahxgyucfpznmtv7pz7lai4msq","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_details":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":301899,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":2655,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":301899},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"20260825065800616465853rw8dpU9b8J9g7","type":"assistant","uuid":"ac27f2df-7b9a-49ac-ab39-567d18b71e17","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:58:34.740Z","effort":"max","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"ac27f2df-7b9a-49ac-ab39-567d18b71e17","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"text":"جدول ۵-۴ **ستون‌هایش جابه‌جا استخراج شد** — طبق §16 حدس نمی‌زنم و آن را `Pending Verification` می‌گذارم. اما متن روایی (که معتبر است) یک نقل تازه و مهم دارد:\n\n> «**All untuned DRL algorithms performed less favourably than the three benchmark optimisation algorithms in the initial simulation.**»\n\nدو تأیید آخر:","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_bdrk_mjgyoc4ivoqnrkbij3vs6ruvaxjahxgyucfpznmtv7pz7lai4msq","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_details":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":301899,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":2655,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":301899},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"20260825065800616465853rw8dpU9b8J9g7","type":"assistant","uuid":"b23dddfd-1cd9-4122-8cc7-6b359d670e17","timestamp":"2026-08-24T22:58:37.814Z","effort":"max","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
