{"parentUuid":"8b57f969-f486-4266-82c1-7e5f432f3e42","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6785fb76-59d2-4c04-ae7f-c3e6eb641817","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Ju1uiMiCcBmgNkGW6PDT81","type":"tool_result","content":"## Bibliographic Details\n\n- **Title:** Deep Reinforcement Learning for Pressure Optimization in Water Distribution Networks with Multiple Pumping Stations\n- **Authors:** Unni Cyriac (first author) and Sibi Chacko (last author) — both affiliated with Heriot-Watt University Malaysia\n- **Publication date:** May 5, 2026 (publication year 2026)\n- **Journal:** *Journal of Water Resources Planning and Management* (American Society of Civil Engineers), vol. 152, issue 7\n- **DOI:** 10.1061/jwrmd5.wreng-7303\n- **Access:** Closed access; 0 citations to date; 15 referenced works\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe record does contain an `abstract_inverted_index`, but a complete word-for-word reconstruction would run roughly 1,700 characters — well past the 125-character quoting limit I work within. Here is a summary instead, with only brief quoted fragments:\n\nThe authors argue that managing pressure in water distribution networks (WDNs) fed by several pressure sources matters for efficiency, asset integrity, and dependable supply. Too much pressure wastes energy and raises the chance of pipe failure and leakage, yet district metered areas (DMAs) still need a floor level to meet service standards. They note that earlier research concentrated on pump efficiency and had \"overlooked using pressure control with RL in continuous action spaces.\"\n\nTheir case study examines a WDN in Abu Dhabi using deep reinforcement learning (DRL). A bespoke training environment was built on the Gym framework, wrapping \"a calibrated hydraulic model of the network.\" The agent learns to vary pumping station setpoints in real time so that peak network pressure is capped while each DMA's minimum requirement holds. Reported outcomes include \"measurable reductions in the energy consumption and the risk of pipe damage\" without sacrificing service reliability. The authors present the workflow as transferable to networks of other sizes and layouts, and — thanks to open-source components — as \"a practical, transferable, and cost-effective solution.\"\n\n## Topics, Keywords, and Concepts\n\n**Primary topic:** Water Systems and Optimization (score 0.999) — subfield: Civil and Structural Engineering; field: Engineering; domain: Physical Sciences. Two other topics appear with negligible scores: Cavitation Phenomena in Pumps and Geotechnical Engineering and Underground Structures.\n\n**Keywords (with scores):** Reinforcement learning (0.66), Energy consumption (0.57), Pressure control (0.51), Work (physics) (0.48), Control (management) (0.44), Energy (signal processing) (0.40), Service (business) (0.38), Pressure regulator (0.34), Hydraulic pressure (0.33).\n\n**Additional assigned concepts:** Computer science, Engineering, Reliability engineering, Efficient energy use, Optimal control, Service level, Control engineering, Sustainability, Automotive engineering, Control system, Water consumption, Consumption (sociology), IT service continuity, Optimization problem, Energy conservation, Pressure system, Operations research, Water pumping.\n\n**Sustainable Development Goal:** Industry, innovation and infrastructure (score 0.44)."}]},"uuid":"0c942669-c2b6-4244-87ed-932f4fc743c0","timestamp":"2026-08-24T23:01:52.447Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":14995,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"## Bibliographic Details\n\n- **Title:** Deep Reinforcement Learning for Pressure Optimization in Water Distribution Networks with Multiple Pumping Stations\n- **Authors:** Unni Cyriac (first author) and Sibi Chacko (last author) — both affiliated with Heriot-Watt University Malaysia\n- **Publication date:** May 5, 2026 (publication year 2026)\n- **Journal:** *Journal of Water Resources Planning and Management* (American Society of Civil Engineers), vol. 152, issue 7\n- **DOI:** 10.1061/jwrmd5.wreng-7303\n- **Access:** Closed access; 0 citations to date; 15 referenced works\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe record does contain an `abstract_inverted_index`, but a complete word-for-word reconstruction would run roughly 1,700 characters — well past the 125-character quoting limit I work within. Here is a summary instead, with only brief quoted fragments:\n\nThe authors argue that managing pressure in water distribution networks (WDNs) fed by several pressure sources matters for efficiency, asset integrity, and dependable supply. Too much pressure wastes energy and raises the chance of pipe failure and leakage, yet district metered areas (DMAs) still need a floor level to meet service standards. They note that earlier research concentrated on pump efficiency and had \"overlooked using pressure control with RL in continuous action spaces.\"\n\nTheir case study examines a WDN in Abu Dhabi using deep reinforcement learning (DRL). A bespoke training environment was built on the Gym framework, wrapping \"a calibrated hydraulic model of the network.\" The agent learns to vary pumping station setpoints in real time so that peak network pressure is capped while each DMA's minimum requirement holds. Reported outcomes include \"measurable reductions in the energy consumption and the risk of pipe damage\" without sacrificing service reliability. The authors present the workflow as transferable to networks of other sizes and layouts, and — thanks to open-source components — as \"a practical, transferable, and cost-effective solution.\"\n\n## Topics, Keywords, and Concepts\n\n**Primary topic:** Water Systems and Optimization (score 0.999) — subfield: Civil and Structural Engineering; field: Engineering; domain: Physical Sciences. Two other topics appear with negligible scores: Cavitation Phenomena in Pumps and Geotechnical Engineering and Underground Structures.\n\n**Keywords (with scores):** Reinforcement learning (0.66), Energy consumption (0.57), Pressure control (0.51), Work (physics) (0.48), Control (management) (0.44), Energy (signal processing) (0.40), Service (business) (0.38), Pressure regulator (0.34), Hydraulic pressure (0.33).\n\n**Additional assigned concepts:** Computer science, Engineering, Reliability engineering, Efficient energy use, Optimal control, Service level, Control engineering, Sustainability, Automotive engineering, Control system, Water consumption, Consumption (sociology), IT service continuity, Optimization problem, Energy conservation, Pressure system, Operations research, Water pumping.\n\n**Sustainable Development Goal:** Industry, innovation and infrastructure (score 0.44).","durationMs":30144,"url":"https://api.openalex.org/works/doi:10.1061/JWRMD5.WRENG-7303"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8b57f969-f486-4266-82c1-7e5f432f3e42","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
