{"parentUuid":"7371e866-8a43-44c1-ad3a-bec5e0e688e8","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6785fb76-59d2-4c04-ae7f-c3e6eb641817","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Doy4k165hXYp3axzFLSkA3","type":"tool_result","content":"## Records Returned\n\nThe query (`multi-algorithm reinforcement learning framework feedforward networks resilient water tank scheduling`) matched 96 works; the page shows 2.\n\n### 1. The role of deep learning in urban water management: A critical review\n- **Authors:** Guangtao Fu (U. Exeter, corresponding), Yiwen Jin (U. Exeter), Siao Sun (Chinese Academy of Sciences / IGSNRR, corresponding), Zhiguo Yuan (U. Queensland), David Butler (U. Exeter)\n- **Journal:** *Water Research*, vol. 223, art. 118973 (Elsevier)\n- **Published:** 11 August 2022\n- **DOI:** https://doi.org/10.1016/j.watres.2022.118973 (PMID 35988335; open access, CC-BY; 420 citations; FWCI 34.06)\n\n**Abstract (decoded from the inverted index and condensed — see note below):** The authors frame deep learning as a \"disruptive technology\" capable of reshaping economies, environments and societies, but argue there is \"lack of a systematic review\" of where it stands in urban water systems. Their review spans demand forecasting, leakage and contamination detection, sewer defect assessment, wastewater system state prediction, asset monitoring, and urban flooding. They conclude the field remains \"still at an early stage,\" since most studies rely on benchmark networks, synthetic data, or laboratory/pilot systems \"with no practical adoption reported.\" Leakage detection is described as \"perhaps at the forefront\" of real day-to-day operational uptake. Five research challenges are named: data privacy, algorithmic development, explainability and trustworthiness, multi-agent systems, and digital twins — the routes toward systems of \"high intelligence and autonomy.\"\n\n### 2. Artificial intelligence and machine learning approaches to energy demand-side response: A systematic review\n- **Authors:** Ioannis Antonopoulos (Heriot-Watt, corresponding), Valentin Robu, Benoit Couraud, Desen Kırlı (U. Edinburgh), Sonam Norbu, Aristides Kiprakis (U. Edinburgh), David Flynn, Sergio Elizondo-Gonzalez (Ofgem), Steve Wattam (Upside Energy)\n- **Journal:** *Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews*, vol. 130, art. 109899 (Elsevier)\n- **Published:** 10 June 2020\n- **DOI:** https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rser.2020.109899 (open access, CC-BY; 631 citations; FWCI 42.45)\n\n**Abstract (decoded and condensed):** Demand Response (DR) is drawing interest as a cost-effective source of flexibility and grid reliability. Because DR tasks are complex, data-intensive, and often require \"near real-time decisions,\" AI and ML have \"recently emerged as key technologies\" for demand-side response. Applications include choosing which consumers to dispatch, learning consumer attributes and preferences, dynamic pricing, device scheduling and control, and designing incentives/rewards that are \"fair and economically efficient.\" The review covers 160+ papers, 40 companies and commercial initiatives, and 21 large-scale projects, classifying papers by both algorithm and DR application area, then discussing advantages, limitations, and future directions.\n\n*Note: I summarized rather than pasted the full abstracts verbatim, keeping each quoted fragment short; the wording above outside quotation marks is my own paraphrase of the decoded index.*\n\n## PPO vs. DQN vs. A3C, Learning-Rate Sensitivity, Noise Robustness\n\n**Not available in this content.** Neither returned record contains any comparison of PPO, DQN, or A3C, any learning-rate sensitivity analysis, or any noise-robustness results. Both are broad review articles rather than algorithm benchmarking studies:\n\n- Record 1 mentions deep learning generally and flags \"multi-agent systems\" as a future challenge, but reports no RL algorithm head-to-head results.\n- Record 2 classifies papers \"with regards to both the AI/ML algorithm(s) used and the application area,\" yet the returned metadata does not surface any named RL algorithms or hyperparameter findings.\n\nNeither record concerns water-tank scheduling or feedforward-network policy architectures. To find the target study, retrieve additional result pages (only 2 of 96 shown, `per_page=2`) or narrow the query — e.g., filter on terms like *water tank* plus *PPO*, *DQN*, and *A3C* directly, or restrict to the reinforcement-learning/control topic rather than the flood-risk and smart-grid topics these two hits fall under."}]},"uuid":"ce51592e-5d4d-41eb-8482-01d146b779ed","timestamp":"2026-08-24T23:04:11.589Z","toolUseResult":{"bytes":74420,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"## Records Returned\n\nThe query (`multi-algorithm reinforcement learning framework feedforward networks resilient water tank scheduling`) matched 96 works; the page shows 2.\n\n### 1. The role of deep learning in urban water management: A critical review\n- **Authors:** Guangtao Fu (U. Exeter, corresponding), Yiwen Jin (U. Exeter), Siao Sun (Chinese Academy of Sciences / IGSNRR, corresponding), Zhiguo Yuan (U. Queensland), David Butler (U. Exeter)\n- **Journal:** *Water Research*, vol. 223, art. 118973 (Elsevier)\n- **Published:** 11 August 2022\n- **DOI:** https://doi.org/10.1016/j.watres.2022.118973 (PMID 35988335; open access, CC-BY; 420 citations; FWCI 34.06)\n\n**Abstract (decoded from the inverted index and condensed — see note below):** The authors frame deep learning as a \"disruptive technology\" capable of reshaping economies, environments and societies, but argue there is \"lack of a systematic review\" of where it stands in urban water systems. Their review spans demand forecasting, leakage and contamination detection, sewer defect assessment, wastewater system state prediction, asset monitoring, and urban flooding. They conclude the field remains \"still at an early stage,\" since most studies rely on benchmark networks, synthetic data, or laboratory/pilot systems \"with no practical adoption reported.\" Leakage detection is described as \"perhaps at the forefront\" of real day-to-day operational uptake. Five research challenges are named: data privacy, algorithmic development, explainability and trustworthiness, multi-agent systems, and digital twins — the routes toward systems of \"high intelligence and autonomy.\"\n\n### 2. Artificial intelligence and machine learning approaches to energy demand-side response: A systematic review\n- **Authors:** Ioannis Antonopoulos (Heriot-Watt, corresponding), Valentin Robu, Benoit Couraud, Desen Kırlı (U. Edinburgh), Sonam Norbu, Aristides Kiprakis (U. Edinburgh), David Flynn, Sergio Elizondo-Gonzalez (Ofgem), Steve Wattam (Upside Energy)\n- **Journal:** *Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews*, vol. 130, art. 109899 (Elsevier)\n- **Published:** 10 June 2020\n- **DOI:** https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rser.2020.109899 (open access, CC-BY; 631 citations; FWCI 42.45)\n\n**Abstract (decoded and condensed):** Demand Response (DR) is drawing interest as a cost-effective source of flexibility and grid reliability. Because DR tasks are complex, data-intensive, and often require \"near real-time decisions,\" AI and ML have \"recently emerged as key technologies\" for demand-side response. Applications include choosing which consumers to dispatch, learning consumer attributes and preferences, dynamic pricing, device scheduling and control, and designing incentives/rewards that are \"fair and economically efficient.\" The review covers 160+ papers, 40 companies and commercial initiatives, and 21 large-scale projects, classifying papers by both algorithm and DR application area, then discussing advantages, limitations, and future directions.\n\n*Note: I summarized rather than pasted the full abstracts verbatim, keeping each quoted fragment short; the wording above outside quotation marks is my own paraphrase of the decoded index.*\n\n## PPO vs. DQN vs. A3C, Learning-Rate Sensitivity, Noise Robustness\n\n**Not available in this content.** Neither returned record contains any comparison of PPO, DQN, or A3C, any learning-rate sensitivity analysis, or any noise-robustness results. Both are broad review articles rather than algorithm benchmarking studies:\n\n- Record 1 mentions deep learning generally and flags \"multi-agent systems\" as a future challenge, but reports no RL algorithm head-to-head results.\n- Record 2 classifies papers \"with regards to both the AI/ML algorithm(s) used and the application area,\" yet the returned metadata does not surface any named RL algorithms or hyperparameter findings.\n\nNeither record concerns water-tank scheduling or feedforward-network policy architectures. To find the target study, retrieve additional result pages (only 2 of 96 shown, `per_page=2`) or narrow the query — e.g., filter on terms like *water tank* plus *PPO*, *DQN*, and *A3C* directly, or restrict to the reinforcement-learning/control topic rather than the flood-risk and smart-grid topics these two hits fall under.","durationMs":87975,"url":"https://api.openalex.org/works?search=multi-algorithm%20reinforcement%20learning%20framework%20feedforward%20networks%20resilient%20water%20tank%20scheduling&per-page=2"},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7371e866-8a43-44c1-ad3a-bec5e0e688e8","session_id":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\Thesis","sessionId":"a4cbf1ac-8d99-42b4-9b8e-e55353a1e1c4","version":"2.1.240","gitBranch":"main"}
